//? Title: SinGAN: One Image, Three Frozen Scales
//? Subtitle: Explicit residual patch games, noise streams and cold per-scale optimizers
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Generate new images from one training image by learning its patches at several
resolutions. A coarse random image supplies structure; finer residual generators
add detail while all completed lower scales stay frozen. This compact native
C++ experiment uses16,24 and32-pixel scales,371 generator and297 discriminator
values per scale, least-squares games and fixed-noise reconstruction. Worked
checks precede the real CIFAR image. The complete program exposes every noise
seed, both warm-start groups, cold Adam states and288 critic-then-fresh-generator
writes. Three fixed epochs per scale reproduce the original arrays and PNGs
exactly. Reconstruction MSE is.1290 and sample pixel variance.006276; neither is
held-out accuracy or evidence of realistic generation.
)
\toc

> Learn local patches from one image at several resolutions

[Rott Shaham, Dekel and Michaeli, SinGAN: Learning a Generative Model from a
Single Natural Image (ICCV2019)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.01164) uses a pyramid
of fully convolutional generators and patch discriminators. This source teaching
variant preserves its coarse-to-fine idea but uses three width4 networks,
three biased convolutions per network and LSGAN losses instead of the paper's
WGAN-GP. It is a small from-scratch experiment, without pretrained weights,
normalization, dropout, generator weight EMA or a held-out image set.

At scale k, upsample the frozen lower-scale draw to p, draw explicit noise z,
and form u=p+a_k z. The generator predicts a bounded correction but the image
itself is not bounded:

$$ G_k(p,z,a_k)=p+\tanh(C_3(\ell(C_2(\ell(C_1(p+a_kz)))))),\qquad
\ell(t)=\max(t,0)+.2\min(t,0). $$
\alttext(The generator adds a tanh correction from three convolutions of previous plus amplitude times noise to the previous image.)

Each3x3 SAME stride1 convolution has a bias and a fan-in uniform kernel.
Widths3→4→4→3 give371 G values in6 leaves. D uses3→4→4→1 and two leaky
activations, giving297 values in6 leaves. D has raw local scores and nominal7x7
receptive fields, with zero padding at boundaries. There is no global pooling
or probability transform. Fixed-width computation is O(NHW) at each scale.

